ade and prosperity  um i mean i think the first thing to say  here anna is that the notion that action  on climate change in the environment is  somehow at odds with prosperity is very  outdated it simply doesn't reflect  market and social dynamics in the 21st  century in the uk alone we've reduced  emissions by roughly 42 while growing  the economy by 78  so the argument simply doesn't stand um  but with reference to the this uh this  leak regarding the trade deal if true  um  it would be a politically baffling move  really i mean concern about climate  change in the environment has never been  higher brits now rate environment and  climate is the second most important  issue facing the country second only to  the covert 19 pandemic so it's not clear  to me whether this is a really a vote  winner  well it's interesting isn't it the  government certainly is playing it down  let's take a look at some of what  they've said in response to this story  they say our ambitious trade deal with  australia will include a substantive  article on climate change in fact  they say that this is a first it's the  first time australia will have ever  included climate provisions in a trade  deal even if those specific temperature  targets aren't included so so what do  you make of that response  well i think the response is is a way of  trying to ameliorate fairly complicated  issues one of them is the fact that  australia measures its own climate  progress in different ways  so they've talked about  going from 26 to 28  decreases in their emissions compared to  a 2005 levels whereas the uk uh talks  about reducing by 68  uh from 1990 levels to 20 30 levels now  there's a lot of complicated mathematics  that goes on within this but nothing  hides the fact that australia houses  79 out of the 176  new coal-fired power stations that are  being set up globally  australia is trying to get out of its  own paris agreement commitments by not  committing appropriately to the paris  government commitments the uk has within  this uh a free trade agreement and  that's wrong the uk should be setting an  example not following laggards  well um seppi i think australian  ministers would say they are committed  to the paris agreement but they may  argue we have got a lot of jobs in the  coal industry and if you're a uk trade  negotiator what are you supposed to do  if you come up against that argument are  you supposed to abandon the trade deal  what would you do  i'd say anna honestly the question is  not which jobs we lose by going net zero  by addressing the paris climate  agreement it's actually which jobs we  lose out on if we don't and there's no  denying that there are jobs in carbon  intensive industries but there are plans  and there will be plans developed to  help retrain and transition uh those  people and i think you know the key  thing to remember here is the world is  moving on already at least two-thirds of  global global gdp is covered by net zero  targets and many businesses are already  getting ahead of this curve and setting  themselves earlier targets bringing jobs  in the process i mean in the uk alone  you know over the summer we saw you know  six thousand jobs for battery tech in  coventry another six thousand two  hundred battery tech in sunderland  another one thousand in eevee production  in cheshire and that's just the tip of  the iceberg and you know we can see  actually net zero breathing life into  britain's former industrial heartlands  and um it's it's really a case of  actually you know seeing the opportunity  here seeing that businesses are  innovating and bringing jobs but  recognizing that there are those that  are affected that needs to be um you  know helped and supported to retrain and  transition on the risk of missing the  point of what's being discussed here  because it's really really important to  focus on the fact that australia is a  nation that hasn't been able to meet um  some of the most obvious uh targets for 